WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.940
Resource protection is part of API security because availability and predictable performance are security properties for real users.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:23.160
An API that accepts unlimited request bodies, returns unbounded result sets, performs expensive work synchronously, or retries slow dependencies without limits can become fragile under normal mistakes as well as hostile traffic.

3
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:32.160
Use request body limits, upload limits, pagination, maximum page sizes, bounded filters, and controlled response sizes.

4
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.860
These controls should reflect business needs and system capacity.

5
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:44.440
A limit is most useful when it is explicit, documented, enforced consistently, and paired with a safe error response that legitimate callers can understand.

6
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.280
Timeouts, cancellation tokens, and graceful handling of slow dependencies help APIs fail predictably.

7
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:57.860
If a client disconnects or a dependency becomes slow, the application should avoid wasting work where possible.

8
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:09.080
Background jobs, long-running operations, and asynchronous workflows need status models, retry rules, and monitoring so they do not become invisible resource drains.

9
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.720
Rate limiting and throttling protect shared capacity.

10
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:21.700
They can be applied by user, client, token, IP range, route, tenant, or operation class depending on the architecture.

11
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:30.200
Rate limits are not a replacement for authorization or input validation, but they help prevent one caller or integration from consuming resources that others depend on.

12
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.640
Expensive queries and repeated operations need design attention.

13
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:43.460
Reporting endpoints, exports, batch jobs, search, file processing, and integrations can all create high resource cost.

14
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:54.920
Bound the inputs, queue heavy work when appropriate, make progress observable, and avoid letting clients trigger uncontrolled loops or memory-heavy processing with untrusted data.

15
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.720
File uploads combine input validation and resource protection.

16
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:07.700
Control file names, paths, content types, size limits, storage locations, scanning or review workflows, and download behavior.

17
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:16.840
Keep uploaded content away from executable paths and treat imported content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated or internal workflow.
